  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Another very warm day across the Tri-State area as \ntemperatures continue to rise into the 80s.  Further west however, a \nfrontal boundary has stalled from Gregory to near Huron and north of \nWatertown.  Northwesterly drainage winds are doing what they like to \ndo and keeping temperatures cooler with considerable stratus through \ncentral and northern South Dakota. This front will stay nearly \nstationary into late afternoon, perhaps sliding a bit further \nsoutheast into the MO River valley. Temperatures will likely fall \nshort of records in most locations, however gusty winds greater than \n25 mph in NW Iowa could promote elevated agricultural related fire \nrisks to sunset. \n\nTONIGHT: My confidence is rising that the cold front will continue \nslipping southeast through the overnight hours, though just how far \nsoutheast is still in question. Should the front reach into NW Iowa \nand NE Nebraska, then a significant amount of cooler air may drain \nsoutheast knocking temperatures further into the upper 40s to lower \n50s. Anticipating stratus to reform late tonight, especially in \nareas from Gregory to Brookings and Marshall where cool air will \ncontinue to drain.\n\nMONDAY: We're likely to start the day with quite a bit of stratus in \nthe CWA, potentially as far SE as Sioux Falls.  A mid-lvl shortwave \ntracking through the Dakotas mid-morning to mid-day will accomplish \ntwo tasks, lifting the cold front very slightly back northwest, but \nalso pinning in cooler and moist air in the drainage flow on the \nbackside of the front.  The end result may be an extreme temperature \ngradient of the 80s in Sioux City and Storm Lake/Spencer, to the 50s \nin Huron.  Latest NBM will not catch these mesoscale temperature \ngradients, so have adjusted and blended in higher resolution \nguidance.  However the gradient may still be more defined than the \ncurrent forecast suggests. While moisture is lacking aloft, a few \nsprinkles to light rain showers could develop through central and \nnortheastern SODAK in the day.  Have also manually pushed up stratus \nthrough mid-day west of I-29.\n\nMONDAY NIGHT: Northwest winds continue through the overnight hours \ninto Tuesday as quite a bit of cooler Canadian air filters southeast \nThis will push overnight lows into the 40s in several areas with \nsome questions on cloud cover potential lingering. \n\nTUESDAY-WEDNESDAY: Latest forecast guidance continues to trend drier \nwith precipitation chances during the middle of the week.  The \namplitude of mid-lvl ridging through the Central and Southern Plains \nmay be strong enough to prevent any cut off energy in the Four \nCorners from moving eastward as quick as guidance hinted yesterday. \nThe end result will be dry conditions Tuesday and Wednesday with \nhighs near or even slightly above seasonal normals. \n\nTHURSDAY-SATURDAY:  The cutoff energy in the Four Corners may \neventually phase with a secondary northern stream trough crossing \nthe Canadian Rockies by Thursday. Increasing warm advection and \nmoisture return ahead of this trough combined with mid-lvl dPVA may \nresult in the development of scattered rain by Thursday.  Latest \nensemble probabilities continue to suggest light rain amounts with \n60-70% probabilities of >0.10\", but only 30% probabilities of more \nthan 0.50\".   High pressure will quickly swing southeast into the \nPlains for Friday and the upcoming weekend. Temperatures for at \nleast a couple days may cool below seasonal normals, but only \nslightly.",
